"""English — details, beginner_2 (50 questions)."""

D = {
"prophets_301": "Zakariyya had prayed, \"My Lord, grant me from Yourself a good offspring\" (3:38), and the angels called to him with the news of Yahya (3:39). He asked how, being old with a barren wife, and was told that Allah does what He wills (3:40). Then he asked for a sign: \"Your sign is that you will not speak to people for three days except by gesture. And remember your Lord much, and exalt Him in the evening and the morning\" (3:41). A Muslim learns that when a prayer is answered, the first reply is thanks and remembrance.",
"prophets_302": "The verse reads: \"And We did not send any messenger except in the language of his people, to make things clear for them. Then Allah sends astray whom He wills and guides whom He wills, and He is the Exalted in Might, the Wise\" (14:4). Surah Ibrahim opens with the Book sent down to bring people out of darkness into light (14:1), and the next verse tells how Musa was sent to his people with the same task (14:5). A Muslim learns that the Qur'an came in Arabic for the same reason: faith is meant to be understood.",
"prophets_303": "The surah opens: \"These are the verses of the clear Book. Indeed, We have sent it down as an Arabic Qur'an that you might understand. We relate to you the best of stories in what We have revealed to you of this Qur'an\" (12:1-3). The story begins at once with the boy Yusuf telling his father of eleven stars, the sun and the moon bowing to him (12:4), and runs without a break to his reunion with his parents in Egypt. Its last verse says these stories hold a lesson for people of understanding (12:111). A Muslim reads the story of Yusuf as one long lesson in patience and trust in Allah.",
"prophets_304": "Surah Maryam turns to Ibrahim with these words: \"And mention in the Book Ibrahim. Indeed, he was a man of truth and a prophet\" (19:41). The verses that follow show both qualities at once: he asks his father gently why he worships what cannot hear, see or help him (19:42), and when his father threatens to stone him, he answers, \"Peace be upon you; I will ask forgiveness for you from my Lord\" (19:47). The Qur'an gives the same title to Idris (19:56) and to Maryam (5:75). A Muslim learns that truthfulness and gentleness belong together.",
"prophets_305": "The Qur'an does not name her; the hoopoe describes a woman ruling over Saba with a great throne, whose people bowed to the sun (27:23-24). Sulaiman sent her a letter that began in the name of Allah and asked her to come in submission (27:30-31); she came, and found her own throne brought before her (27:42). Then: \"She said, 'My Lord, I have wronged myself, and I submit with Sulaiman to Allah, Lord of the worlds'\" (27:44). A Muslim learns that turning to Allah begins with admitting one's own wrong.",
"prophets_306": "The verse gives the whole exchange: \"And when Ibrahim said, 'My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead.' He said, 'Have you not believed?' He said, 'Yes, but so that my heart may be at rest'\" (2:260). Allah told him to take four birds, place a part of them on each hill, then call them, and they would come to him in haste. The verse just before tells of a man made to die for a hundred years and then raised (2:259). A Muslim learns that asking Allah to strengthen the heart is a prayer of faith, not of doubt.",
"prophets_307": "The journey begins with a promise: \"And when Musa said to his servant, 'I will not cease until I reach the junction of the two seas, or I will go on for a long time'\" (18:60). At a rock they forgot their fish, which found its way into the sea; Musa said, \"That is what we were seeking,\" and they went back on their own footprints (18:61-64). There they found \"a servant from among Our servants to whom We had given mercy from Us and taught knowledge from Us\" (18:65), and Musa asked to follow him and be taught (18:66). A Muslim learns that a prophet who spoke with Allah still travelled far and asked humbly to learn.",
"prophets_308": "The four verses read: \"Say: He is Allah, One. Allah, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born, nor is there to Him any equivalent\" (112:1-4). The surah opens with the command \"Say\", given to the Prophet ﷺ so that he would declare who Allah is to those who asked. Each verse closes a door: no partner, no need, no parent, no child, no likeness, as the Qur'an says elsewhere, \"There is nothing like unto Him\" (42:11). A Muslim recites these verses in prayer and learns from them the plainest statement of who he worships.",
"prophets_309": "Every standard biography records that, long before revelation, the people of Makkah called him al-Amin, the trustworthy, and left their valuables in his keeping. When Quraysh rebuilt the Ka'bah and quarrelled over who would set the Black Stone in place, they agreed that the next man to enter would decide; it was he, and they said, \"This is al-Amin.\" He laid the stone on a cloak, had a chief of each clan lift a corner, and set it in place himself. A Muslim learns that his honesty was known to his people before his message was.",
"prophets_310": "The verse reads: \"There has certainly been for you in the Messenger of Allah an excellent pattern for anyone whose hope is in Allah and the Last Day and who remembers Allah often\" (33:21). It stands in the account of the Battle of the Confederates, when enemies came from above and below and hearts reached the throats (33:10). The Prophet ﷺ was in the trench with the believers, who, seeing the armies, said, \"This is what Allah and His Messenger promised us\" (33:22). A Muslim learns to follow him in patience under hardship as much as in prayer and kindness.",
"sira_251": "Every standard biography records that he was born in Makkah, in the clan of Banu Hashim of Quraysh, the tribe that kept the Ka'bah. His father 'Abdullah had died before his birth, and the Qur'an recalls it: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\" (93:6). The Qur'an calls Makkah the Mother of Cities (6:92), swears by \"this secure city\" (95:3), and names it the place of the first House built for mankind (3:96). A Muslim learns that the last Prophet ﷺ was born beside the House that Ibrahim raised.",
"sira_252": "Every standard biography records that when Quraysh planned to kill him, the Prophet ﷺ left Makkah with Abu Bakr and travelled to Yathrib, where the tribes of Aws and Khazraj had pledged to protect him as they protected their own families. The Qur'an recalls the journey: \"If you do not aid him, Allah has already aided him, when those who disbelieved drove him out, the second of two, when they were in the cave\" (9:40). From that year the town was called the City of the Prophet. A Muslim learns that Madinah became the home of Islam because a people opened their doors to a persecuted messenger.",
"sira_253": "The word hijra means leaving one place for another; every standard biography uses it for the departure of the Prophet ﷺ and his companions from Makkah to Madinah, and in the caliphate of 'Umar the years of the Muslim calendar were counted from that event. The Qur'an speaks of it as done for Allah: \"And whoever emigrates for the cause of Allah will find on the earth many locations and abundance\" (4:100). It calls those who emigrated and those who sheltered them the true believers (8:74). A Muslim learns that the calendar begins not with a birth or a victory but with a sacrifice made for faith.",
"sira_254": "Every standard biography records that Khadija bint Khuwaylid, a merchant of Quraysh, first employed the Prophet ﷺ to carry her goods to Syria and then, admiring his honesty, proposed marriage to him. When he came down from the cave of Hira shaken by the first revelation, she wrapped him in a cloak and told him that Allah would never disgrace him, because he kept ties with his family, carried the burdens of others and helped the poor. She stood by him until her death in Makkah, before the Hijra. A Muslim learns that the first to accept Islam was a wife who knew her husband's character better than anyone.",
"sira_255": "Every standard biography records that Thawr is a mountain south of Makkah, in the opposite direction from Madinah, chosen so that the searchers would look the wrong way; Quraysh still came so close that Abu Bakr feared they would be seen. The Qur'an tells what was said inside: \"when he said to his companion, 'Do not grieve; indeed, Allah is with us.' And Allah sent down His tranquillity upon him and supported him with soldiers you did not see\" (9:40). After three nights they set out with a guide along the coast road. A Muslim learns that careful planning and complete trust in Allah went together in the Prophet's ﷺ own journey.",
"sira_256": "The first verses read: \"Read in the name of your Lord who created, created man from a clinging clot. Read, and your Lord is the Most Generous, who taught by the pen, taught man what he did not know\" (96:1-5). Every standard biography records that they came in the cave of Hira, on the mountain outside Makkah where the Prophet ﷺ went alone to worship, when the angel Jibril appeared and told him to read; he answered that he was not a reader, and the angel repeated the command until the verses came. A Muslim learns that Islam began with a call to knowledge, and that the pen is named as the tool of teaching.",
"sira_257": "The Qur'an names him: \"Say, whoever is an enemy to Jibril, it is he who has brought it down upon your heart by permission of Allah\" (2:97), and calls him \"the Trustworthy Spirit\" who brought the Qur'an down upon the heart of the Prophet ﷺ (26:193-194). Surah At-Takwir describes him as a noble messenger, possessed of power, secure with the Owner of the Throne, obeyed and trustworthy (81:19-21). Every standard biography records that he first came to the Prophet ﷺ in the cave of Hira. A Muslim learns that the Qur'an reached the Prophet ﷺ through an angel Allah Himself calls trustworthy.",
"sira_258": "Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ died in Madinah, in the room of his wife 'Aisha beside his mosque, and was buried in that same room. Abu Bakr and 'Umar were later buried next to him, and the mosque was extended until it enclosed the room; the green dome above it is a landmark of Madinah today. On the day he died, Abu Bakr recited to the stunned Muslims: \"Muhammad is not but a messenger; messengers have passed on before him\" (3:144). A Muslim who visits Madinah greets him at his grave with peace, and learns that even the best of creation returned to Allah.",
"sira_259": "The Qur'an has a surah named after the tribe: \"For the accustomed security of Quraysh, their security in the journey of winter and summer, let them worship the Lord of this House, who has fed them against hunger and made them safe from fear\" (106:1-4). Quraysh lived around the Ka'bah, kept the House, served the pilgrims and traded by two yearly caravans. Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ belonged to its clan of Banu Hashim, and that the same tribe became his fiercest opponent before it accepted Islam. A Muslim learns that the message was addressed first to the Prophet's ﷺ own people.",
"sira_260": "The word sahabi comes from sahib, companion, the very word the Qur'an uses for Abu Bakr in the cave: \"when he said to his companion, 'Do not grieve'\" (9:40). The Qur'an describes them together: \"Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and those with him are firm against the disbelievers, merciful among themselves; you see them bowing and prostrating\" (48:29), and says of the first Muhajirun and Ansar that \"Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him\" (9:100). A Muslim says \"may Allah be pleased with him\" after a companion's name, and learns the religion through what they passed on.",
"sira_261": "Every standard biography records that Abu Bakr had kept two camels ready for months, and that the Prophet ﷺ came to his house at midday to tell him the time had come. His daughter Asma brought food to the cave, and his son 'Abdullah brought news of what Quraysh were saying. The Qur'an calls him simply the second of two: \"when those who disbelieved drove him out, the second of two, when they were in the cave, when he said to his companion, 'Do not grieve; indeed, Allah is with us'\" (9:40). A Muslim learns that Abu Bakr's place at the Prophet's ﷺ side is written in the Qur'an itself.",
"sira_262": "Every standard biography records that Bilal ibn Rabah was an enslaved man of Abyssinian origin in Makkah, tortured under the sun by his master for believing, and that he kept repeating \"Ahad, Ahad\", One, One, until Abu Bakr bought him and set him free. In Madinah, when the call to prayer was established, the Prophet ﷺ chose Bilal to give it, and on the day Makkah was opened Bilal climbed onto the Ka'bah and called the adhan from its roof. The Qur'an says the noblest before Allah is the most God-fearing (49:13). A Muslim learns that the voice that called the first believers to prayer belonged to a man once bought and sold.",
"sira_263": "The Qur'an names the place: \"And already had Allah given you victory at Badr while you were few in number, so fear Allah that you may be grateful\" (3:123). Surah Al-Anfal tells how the believers called on their Lord and He answered, \"I will reinforce you with a thousand of the angels, following one another\" (8:9), and calls that day \"the day of the criterion, the day when the two armies met\" (8:41). Every standard biography records that the Muslims went out to meet a caravan and found an army from Makkah instead. A Muslim learns that victory at Badr came from Allah, not from numbers.",
"sira_264": "The Qur'an uses the name itself: \"The first forerunners among the Muhajirun and the Ansar, and those who followed them in good conduct: Allah is pleased with them and they are pleased with Him\" (9:100). Surah Al-Hashr describes what they gave up: \"the poor emigrants who were expelled from their homes and their properties, seeking bounty from Allah and His approval, and supporting Allah and His Messenger; those are the truthful\" (59:8). They left houses, trade and family in Makkah with nothing but their faith. A Muslim learns that leaving what is dear for the sake of Allah earned these companions a name the Qur'an records with praise.",
"sira_265": "The name means \"the helpers\", and the Qur'an describes them right after the emigrants: \"And those who were settled in the home and in faith before them love those who emigrated to them, and find in their hearts no need for what they were given, and give preference over themselves even though they are in want\" (59:9). Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ paired each emigrant with a brother from the Ansar, who shared his house and his livelihood. The same word is used for the disciples of 'Isa, \"the helpers of Allah\" (61:14). A Muslim learns that receiving a stranger as a brother is one of the acts the Qur'an praises most warmly.",
"sira_266": "Every standard biography records that when the Prophet ﷺ entered Madinah, his camel knelt on an open plot used for drying dates, and he bought the land from its owners rather than accept it as a gift. He built the mosque there with walls of mud brick, pillars of palm trunks and a roof of palm branches, carrying the bricks with his own hands, and his family's rooms opened onto its courtyard. The Qur'an says of such houses that Allah has ordered them to be raised and His name remembered in them morning and evening (24:36). A Muslim learns that the first foundation of the new community was a place to pray together.",
"sira_267": "Every standard biography records that Fatima was the daughter of the Prophet ﷺ and Khadija, that she married 'Ali ibn Abi Talib in Madinah, and that their sons al-Hasan and al-Husayn were the grandsons the Prophet ﷺ carried and played with. The Prophet ﷺ said that Fatima was a part of him, and that whoever angered her angered him. She outlived her father by only a few months and was the first of his household to follow him. A Muslim learns that the household of the Prophet ﷺ is honoured, and that Fatima and 'Ali are the parents of his line.",
"sira_268": "Every standard biography records that during his last illness the Prophet ﷺ told Abu Bakr to lead the Muslims in prayer, and that on the day of his death Abu Bakr steadied the weeping companions by reciting: \"Muhammad is not but a messenger; messengers have passed on before him. So if he dies or is killed, will you turn back on your heels?\" (3:144). The Muslims then gave him their pledge as khalifa, a word meaning the one who comes after. A Muslim learns that the first leader after the Prophet ﷺ was the companion who had stood beside him in the cave (9:40).",
"sira_269": "The Qur'an uses both names. During the siege of the Trench, the hypocrites cried, \"O people of Yathrib, there is no place for you here, so go back\" (33:13); elsewhere it speaks of \"the people of Madinah and the bedouins around them\" (9:120). Every standard biography records that once the Prophet ﷺ settled in Yathrib, the town took the name Madinat an-Nabi, the City of the Prophet, and then simply al-Madinah, the City. A Muslim learns that a town was renamed for the man it sheltered, and calls it by that name to this day.",
"sira_270": "Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ was forty years old when the angel Jibril came to him in the cave of Hira with the first verses of Surah Al-'Alaq. Before that he had spent years in Makkah trading honestly, raising a family with Khadija and going out to the cave alone to worship; after it he spent the rest of his life carrying the message. The Qur'an names forty as the age of full strength, when a person says, \"My Lord, enable me to be grateful for Your favour\" (46:15). A Muslim learns that a whole life of good character came before the first word of revelation.",
"sira_271": "The Qur'an says: \"Indeed, the first House established for mankind was that at Bakkah, blessed and a guidance for the worlds\" (3:96), Bakkah being another name of Makkah. It tells how Ibrahim and Isma'il raised its foundations, praying, \"Our Lord, accept this from us\" (2:127), and it calls the Ka'bah \"the Sacred House, a standing for the people\" (5:97). The House stands in the open court of the Sacred Mosque, and pilgrims walk around it seven times. A Muslim learns that the black-draped building at the heart of Makkah is the oldest house of worship, raised by a prophet and his son.",
"sira_272": "The Qur'an names it in the first verse of Surah Al-Isra: \"Exalted is He who took His Servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed, to show him of Our signs\" (17:1). The Farthest Mosque is al-Masjid al-Aqsa in Jerusalem, and the verse records the Night Journey of the Prophet ﷺ from Makkah to that city. Every standard biography records that in Madinah the Muslims prayed toward Jerusalem until the Qur'an turned them toward the Ka'bah (2:144). A Muslim learns that a city far from Makkah is bound to his prayer and to his Prophet's ﷺ story.",
"sira_273": "Every standard biography records that Abu Talib, brother of the Prophet's ﷺ father 'Abdullah, took the orphan boy into his home after the death of their father 'Abd al-Muttalib, and took him along on a trading journey to Syria. When the message came, Abu Talib refused every demand of Quraysh to hand over his nephew, and when Quraysh boycotted Banu Hashim, he went with his clan into the mountain pass and shared their hunger. He died in Makkah in the same year as Khadija, known as the Year of Sorrow. A Muslim learns that Allah placed protection for His Messenger in the loyalty of an uncle.",
"sira_274": "Every standard biography records that Khadija bint Khuwaylid owned goods that she sent out for trade with the caravans of Quraysh, hiring men to sell them and sharing the profit. Hearing of the young man's honesty, she employed Muhammad ﷺ to take her goods to Syria with her servant Maysara, who returned with a good profit and with praise for the way he had dealt. That honesty in business led her to propose marriage, and her wealth later supported him through the first years of the message. A Muslim learns that fair trade is part of good character, and that the Prophet's ﷺ first supporter met him through it.",
"sira_275": "Every standard biography records that in the tenth year after the Hijra the Prophet ﷺ led the pilgrimage himself, the only Hajj he made from Madinah, and preached to the pilgrims at 'Arafah. He told them he did not know whether he would meet them there after that year, that their lives and property were inviolable to one another, and asked whether he had conveyed the message; when they said yes, he called Allah to witness. On that pilgrimage the verse came down: \"This day I have perfected for you your religion and completed My favour upon you\" (5:3). A Muslim learns that the last sermon of the Prophet ﷺ was his farewell, and that his message was complete.",
"sira_276": "The word sira means a way of going, and so a person's course through life; every standard biography of the Prophet ﷺ, from the earliest written account of Ibn Ishaq onward, is called by this name. The Qur'an holds parts of the story: his orphanhood, \"Did He not find you an orphan and give you shelter?\" (93:6); the cave of the Hijra (9:40); victory at Badr when the believers were few (3:123); the fear of the Trench (33:10); and the opening of Makkah (110:1-2). The Sira arranges these into one life from birth to death. A Muslim reads it to know the man he is told to take as his pattern (33:21).",
"sira_277": "The word hadith means a report or a piece of speech; each hadith gives first the chain of those who passed it on, then the text itself, so a reader can see who heard it from whom. In Sahih al-Bukhari 8, for example, Ibn 'Umar reports that the Prophet ﷺ said Islam is built on five. The hadith matters because the Qur'an commands: \"Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it; and whatever he forbids you, refrain from it\" (59:7). Through the hadith a Muslim knows how the Prophet ﷺ prayed, ate, greeted and spoke. A Muslim learns that the Qur'an and the hadith are read together, one as the Book and the other as its living example.",
"sira_278": "Every standard account records that Imam al-Bukhari, Muhammad ibn Isma'il, and his younger contemporary Imam Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj, both in the third century after the Hijra, each gathered only those reports whose chain of narrators was unbroken and whose every narrator was known to be trustworthy and accurate. The word sahih means sound. A hadith found in both books is called \"agreed upon\", the highest rank a report can hold; the hadith that Islam is built on five, Sahih al-Bukhari 8, is one of them. A Muslim learns that careful checking of who said what is a duty the community took seriously from its earliest centuries.",
"sira_279": "Every standard account records that the Qur'an was first gathered onto sheets in the caliphate of Abu Bakr, by Zayd ibn Thabit, after many reciters had been killed in battle, and that those sheets were kept by Hafsa, the Prophet's ﷺ widow. In the caliphate of 'Uthman, Hudhayfa ibn al-Yaman returned from the wars and warned that Muslims in the provinces were disputing over how to recite. 'Uthman borrowed Hafsa's sheets, had Zayd and other companions copy them, and sent a copy to each main city so that all read from one text. A Muslim learns that the text he reads today is the one 'Uthman sent out, and that Allah promised to guard it (15:9).",
"sira_280": "Every standard account names them in the order they ruled: Abu Bakr, then 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, then 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, then 'Ali ibn Abi Talib. All four were among the earliest Muslims and prayed behind the Prophet ﷺ, and all four were tied to him by marriage: Abu Bakr and 'Umar were the fathers of his wives 'Aisha and Hafsa, while 'Uthman and 'Ali married his daughters. They are called rashidun, rightly guided, because they governed by the Qur'an and the Prophet's ﷺ practice. A Muslim learns their four names as the leaders who kept the community together after the Prophet ﷺ.",
"sira_281": "The Qur'an records the moment the direction was fixed: \"We have certainly seen the turning of your face toward the heaven, and We will surely turn you to a qibla with which you will be pleased. So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque. And wherever you are, turn your faces toward it\" (2:144). Every standard biography records that in Madinah the Muslims had prayed toward Jerusalem until this verse came. The command is repeated, \"wherever you may be\" (2:150), so that a Muslim in any country faces the same point. A Muslim learns that turning toward the Ka'bah is obedience to this verse and a sign of one community.",
"sira_282": "The Qur'an uses the word itself in the verses about the change of direction: \"The foolish among the people will say, 'What has turned them away from their qibla, which they used to face?' Say, 'To Allah belong the east and the west'\" (2:142). The next verse explains that the first qibla was a test to show who would follow the Messenger ﷺ (2:143), and then comes the command to face the Sacred Mosque (2:144). The word comes from a root meaning to face or turn toward. A Muslim learns that the qibla is not a holy direction in itself but the direction Allah chose, and that facing it is following Him.",
"sira_283": "The Arabic word masjid comes from sajada, to prostrate, so a mosque is literally a place of prostration. The Qur'an says: \"The mosques are for Allah, so do not invoke with Allah anyone\" (72:18), and that they are maintained by those who believe in Allah and the Last Day, establish prayer and give zakat (9:18). It describes them as houses Allah has permitted to be raised, where His name is remembered morning and evening (24:36). Every standard biography records that the first thing the Prophet ﷺ built in Madinah was his mosque. A Muslim learns that a mosque is defined by what happens in it: prostration to Allah alone.",
"sira_284": "Every standard biography records that when the Muslims in Madinah needed a way to gather for prayer, the Prophet ﷺ established the adhan, and Bilal became the first to call it. Its words are heard five times a day wherever Muslims live: Allah is greatest; I bear witness that there is no god but Allah; I bear witness that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; come to prayer; come to success; Allah is greatest; there is no god but Allah. The Qur'an speaks of the call: \"When the call is made for prayer on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah\" (62:9). A Muslim learns to answer the adhan by repeating its words and then going to pray.",
"sira_285": "The Qur'an commands it: \"O you who have believed, when the call is made for prayer on the day of Jumu'ah, then proceed to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade. That is better for you, if you only knew. And when the prayer has been concluded, disperse within the land and seek from the bounty of Allah\" (62:9-10). The name jumu'ah means gathering, and the surah that holds these verses is named after it. In every Muslim town the Friday prayer replaces the midday prayer, with a sermon before it heard in silence. A Muslim learns that once a week the shops close, the community stands in rows together, and then goes back to work.",
"sira_286": "The word imam means the one in front, whom others follow; the Qur'an gives it to Ibrahim, \"Indeed, I will make you a leader for the people\" (2:124), and the believers pray, \"Make us a leader for the righteous\" (25:74). In the prayer the imam stands ahead of the rows, and the congregation bows and prostrates when he does. Every standard biography records that the Prophet ﷺ led the prayers in his mosque, and that in his final illness he ordered Abu Bakr to lead in his place. A Muslim learns that praying behind an imam is following one voice together, as the companions followed the Prophet ﷺ.",
"sira_287": "The word hafiz means one who keeps and guards; the Qur'an uses the same root when Allah says of the Book: \"Indeed, it is We who sent down the Qur'an, and indeed, We will be its guardian\" (15:9). It also says: \"We have certainly made the Qur'an easy for remembrance, so is there any who will remember?\" (54:17). Every standard biography records that the companions learned the verses by heart as they came down, and that the Prophet ﷺ reviewed the whole Qur'an with Jibril every Ramadan. A Muslim learns that memorising the Qur'an is one of the ways Allah keeps His promise to guard it.",
"sira_288": "Every standard account records that Abu Hurayra came to Madinah from the tribe of Daws, embraced Islam and attached himself to the Prophet ﷺ, living among the poor companions who slept in the mosque and following him constantly to hear what he said. He himself explained that while the Muhajirun were busy in the markets and the Ansar with their land, he stayed close and remembered. He was called father of the kitten after a small cat he kept. A Muslim learns that a great part of what the community knows of the Prophet's ﷺ words came through one attentive listener.",
"sira_289": "Every standard biography records that Abu Bakr believed the moment the Prophet ﷺ told him of the message, and that when Quraysh mocked the Night Journey to Jerusalem, he answered that if the Prophet ﷺ had said it, it was true, since he already believed him about news that came from heaven; from then he was called as-Siddiq. The word means one who affirms the truth without hesitation, and the Qur'an gives it to Ibrahim (19:41) and to Maryam (5:75). The Qur'an calls him the second of two in the cave (9:40). A Muslim learns that Abu Bakr's title was earned by believing at once what others took years to accept.",
"sira_290": "Every standard biography records that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab had been a fierce opponent of Islam in Makkah, and that his acceptance of the faith changed the position of the Muslims: after it they prayed openly at the Ka'bah for the first time. Al-Faruq means the one who separates, and 'Umar was known for a justice that drew a sharp line between right and wrong. He was chosen by Abu Bakr to succeed him, ruled as the second caliph, and it was in his time that the Muslim calendar was dated from the Hijra. A Muslim learns that strength placed in the service of truth becomes justice, and that 'Umar's title records it.",
}
